>
  <p>
    Victoria has some of Australia's most accessible overnight hiking, but
    "accessible" does not mean unregulated. Most hike-in campsites need a
    booking made in advance through Parks Victoria, and several popular routes
    cap group sizes or limit how many consecutive nights you can stay.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide is a planning tool, not a track report. We link every route,
    distance and booking rule to its Parks Victoria (or relevant park authority)
    source page so you can check current conditions before you go.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Almost every popular overnight hike-in campsite in Victoria needs a
        booking through <a
          href="https://www.parkstay.vic.gov.au/"
          rel="noopener">ParkStay Victoria</a
        > before you leave home.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sealers Cove at Wilsons Promontory is graded a challenging Grade 4 day
        walk on its own, so treat it as an intermediate first overnighter, not a
        beginner one.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Great Ocean Walk's shorter legs, like Apollo Bay to Elliot Ridge,
        are a gentler way to try an overnight hike near Melbourne.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cathedral Range's Farmyard camping area needs no booking or fee, which
        makes it a low-friction way to test your <a
          href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list">overnight kit</a
        > close to home.
      </li>
      <li>
        Always check current fire ratings and track closures before you go.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

