  <h2>What overnight hikes in Victoria need a booking?</h2>
  <p>
    Most Parks Victoria hike-in campsites now require an online booking that
    doubles as your camping permit. Wilsons Promontory's southern circuit
    campsites, including Sealers Cove and Refuge Cove, need <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/sites/sealers-cove-hike-in-camping-area"
      rel="noopener">advance bookings</a
    >, and the <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/parks/wilsons-promontory-national-park/where-to-stay/southern-overnight-hikes"
      rel="noopener">Southern Circuit page</a
    > confirms a mandatory booking, a maximum of two consecutive nights per site and
    a 12-person group cap. The Great Ocean Walk's hike-in campgrounds work the same
    way: a valid booking through ParkStay "acts as your permit to camp," booked per
    campsite per night.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not every site charges or gates entry, though. Cathedral Range State Park's
    Farmyard camping area is a rare exception: Parks Victoria's own <a
      href="https://www.parks.vic.gov.au/places-to-see/parks/cathedral-range-state-park/where-to-stay/camping-in-cathedral-range"
      rel="noopener">camping page</a
    > states plainly that Farmyard needs no bookings and no fee, unlike the park's
    two car-based campgrounds nearby. Check the specific site page for your chosen
    park before assuming either way.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes near Melbourne?</h2>
  <p>
    "Beginner overnight hike" is relative: even the gentler routes below involve
    a full pack, an unpowered campsite and several hours of walking. Distances
    are one-way and taken directly from each park authority's current site page.
  </p>
